From 184660eb96dedf1a95aae226d6c8e027e8d020e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 04:43:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(shell): recognize arithmetic backticks, literal
 apostrophes and $$ [daveey]

Follow-up to PR #2826 review (Kanban t_01a1d851a3e9e086). Three fail-open
gaps in tools/shell_arith_sink.substitution_in_arithmetic, each reproduced
with real bash 5.2 (a marker command ran) while the scanner returned False:

1. `$${` (PID `$$` followed by a literal `{`) was tokenized as a `${`
   opener. Since #2826 dropped the _PARAM recovery on `)`/`]`/`]]`, that
   phantom frame only closed on `}` and hid every later sink
   (`a[$(...)]=1`, `[[ 1 -eq $(...) ]]`). Fix: tokenize `$$` before `${`.
2. Backtick substitution inside arithmetic (`$(( 1 + `...` ))`, and in a
   parameter word `${v:-`...`}` feeding arithmetic) was not a sink. Fix:
   a backtick inside an _ARITH/_PARAM_ARITH frame returns True.
3. Single quotes are literal inside `$(( ))` (double-quote context), so
   `$(( '0+$(cmd)' ))` and `${v:-'0+$(cmd)'}` in arithmetic run the
   substitution; the scanner skipped the span as data. Fix: apostrophes
   are not quote openers when the nearest evaluator is arithmetic
   expansion (`let` and `[[ ]]` still parse shell words first).

Proof (worker-8, scripts/run_tests.sh):
- RED: new tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_lexical.py against
  origin/main 20c99f7a67's scanner: 9 failed / 11 passed (all 7 real-bash
  marker cases plus 2 backtick sink cases).
- GREEN: same file on this head passes; 36-file lifecycle/shell family
  2683 passed, 0 failed; tests/cron/test_shell_guard_mutation_regressions.py
  5 passed; scripts/check --base origin/main: 20 checks ok.
- Mutation: reverting each of the `$$` token, the arithmetic-quote rule,
  the shell-word exemption and the backtick rule makes the new tests fail.
